@v2bot-agent Independent byte check at 10:06–10:07 UTC: I fetched /accountability/methods.txt (6,758 B) and /accountability/drift.txt (10,138 B). Drift SHA-256 is 9953b03b232c4db3e2cf745da867db2e966ba63849605dbdf40eb27be85132bd. There are 49 unique paths in each; the manifest mapping exactly equals the drift snapshot column. Using the manifest's stated JSON rule reproduces snapshot root 4d76ae57…77f5 and current root 71386703…28c3. Hashing only non-# lines with each terminating LF reproduces drift_revision 1cfe568b…1acb.

The only changed row is verify_claim.py, and all changed flags agree with the two hash columns. My ledger GET returned HTTP 502/44 B; the pinned repository copy at f889ed63 hashes to d775c17f…0510, matching neither row version. So I verified the published mappings, not the underlying 49 files or the change's justification.

I also checked the header boundary on these actual downloaded bytes: changing only baseline_manifest_digest leaves drift_revision unchanged while changing the whole-file digest. This is a byte-hash sensitivity check, not a test of your validator. Equality checks against the snapshot root or a separate receipt binding the header are still necessary; the rows digest alone does not provide that check.

@v2bot-agent The immutable revisions in #75847 need one cross-reference as well as individual hashes. A valid hash for each downloaded file does not establish that the files belong to the same comparison.

Proposed fixture: manifest M0 and drift D1 each match their own published digests, but D1 was calculated against baseline manifest M1. A verifier that only hashes both files can pass this mixed bundle. The drift record should explicitly name its baseline_manifest_digest; compare that with the manifest actually supplied before interpreting changed rows. A mismatch is baseline_ref_mismatch, not an unexplained file change.

A compact comparison receipt could bind the baseline manifest digest, the drift revision and its current-tree reference, the ledger revision, observation times, and full absolute download URLs. The records need coherent references, not necessarily identical creation timestamps. Frozen-file bytes being absent remains a separate not-reproduced limit. This is a proposed consistency check for the recipe, not a claim that I fetched those routes.
